// The world a tau function is compiled to.
//
// # Why this and not wasi:http/incoming-handler
//
// A tau invocation is not always HTTP. A pubsub delivery and a channel call are
// invocations too, and pkg/invoke's Request says so in as many words — so a
// world that could only carry an HTTP exchange would make the other two
// second-class at the ABI, which is the one place that is expensive to undo.
//
// This world mirrors that Request exactly, which is what lets one tier serve
// every trigger. Supporting `wasi:http/incoming-handler` as a SECOND world is
// worth doing — it is what makes a component built for wasmtime serve, Spin or
// wasmCloud run here unmodified — and it is additive: a component exporting it
// is recognised by what it exports, not by a flag.
//
// # One package per capability, and this world is the union
//
// Every capability is its own package under deps/, with its own version and its
// own world. This world imports each of their interfaces, so what a tau function
// may reach is this list and nothing else.
//
// THE SPLIT IS NOT TIDINESS. Three things fall out of it:
//
//   - A capability versions on its own. Adding a field to `storage` moved the
//     version every cache guest was built against, back when there was one
//     package.
//
//   - A SUBSET BECOMES EXPRESSIBLE. `implements:` already names worlds beyond
//     the handler — an authorizer, a router, a gateway transformer — and each
//     serves a different subset of these. A subset is a list of packages, and
//     there was nothing to pick from when the package was the whole set. Each
//     capability also ships a `world imports` of its own, so a guest that needs
//     one of them can target that world alone.
//
//   - The host half matches it one to one. pkg/invoke/wasm registers one host
//     component per package (host.go), so a capability is a file on each side
//     rather than two more lines in an instantiation.
//
// # A guest imports only what it calls, and that is measured
//
// A component's import section names the interfaces it actually reaches, not
// the ones its world offered: fixtures/handler.wasm is built against this world
// and imports NONE of these, while fixtures/cacher.wasm imports the ones it
// calls. So offering every capability costs a guest that uses one of them
// nothing at all.
//
// # These are `import`s and not `include`s, and the reason is a toolchain
//
// `include taubyte:cache/imports@0.1.0;` says the same thing more compactly and
// resolves under wasm-tools 1.253. cargo-component 0.21's wit-parser does not
// resolve a cross-package world include from a `deps/` tree at all, and the
// error it gives names the package rather than the include — so the shape that
// costs a guest author an afternoon is not worth the four saved lines.
//
// # The export is named by the author
//
// `execution/call` in the project's configuration names the export, so a
// project with two functions in one component is two exports rather than two
// components. That is why this world declares the SHAPE and not the name: the
// host calls whatever the config says, and what it requires is the signature.
package taubyte:handler@0.1.0;

world handler {
    // The four data models the `database` kind declares, each its own package
    // because they share nothing but the kind's name.
    import taubyte:sql/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:kv/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:document/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:vector/store@0.1.0;

    import taubyte:cache/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:storage/objects@0.1.0;

    // Sending: three kinds, three deliveries, and every one of the matching
    // RECEIVE halves is a trigger rather than a function a guest calls.
    import taubyte:%stream/publisher@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:bus/publisher@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:queue/producer@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:log/records@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:workflow/runs@0.1.0;

    import taubyte:secret/vault@0.1.0;

    // In-project addressing: what v1 called p2p.
    import taubyte:channel/dialer@0.1.0;

    // And what the workload is. The one capability that is authority over
    // nothing, and so the one that needs no grant. v1 called it `self`, which
    // is a keyword in Rust and cannot be a package name there.
    import taubyte:workload/identity@0.1.0;

    // THE REQUEST AND RESPONSE TYPES ARE IN THE WORLD AND NOT IN AN INTERFACE,
    // which is not a style choice: a `use` from an imported interface leaves
    // the export's signature referring to a type the host has to resolve
    // separately, and a host reading the component's own type section then
    // cannot flatten the call at all. Inline, the export carries its whole
    // shape with it — which is what lets any embedder call it knowing only this
    // file.

    // A header, once. It is a list of pairs rather than a map because a header
    // may legitimately appear more than once — Set-Cookie is the example that
    // breaks every map-shaped API.
    record header {
        name: string,
        value: string,
    }

    // What a guest is handed.
    //
    // Method and path are empty for an invocation that is not HTTP-shaped,
    // which is a case a header list and a body still describe perfectly well:
    // a pubsub delivery is a body and some metadata.
    record request {
        method: string,
        path: string,
        headers: list<header>,
        body: list<u8>,
    }

    // What a guest answers with.
    //
    // A guest that fails does so by returning a status, not by trapping: a trap
    // takes the instance with it, and an instance is warm state worth keeping
    // across a handled error.
    record response {
        status: u16,
        headers: list<header>,
        body: list<u8>,
    }

    // The name is the author's — `execution/call` — and this declares the
    // signature every one of them has.
    export handle: func(req: request) -> response;
}
